    "explanation": "Baseline agg_gbc_34 (34 aggregated motion features): val macro-F1 0.826, test macro-F1 0.679 (test accuracy 0.707). This reproduces the shipped model (ml/reports/metrics.json: test macro-F1 0.672, accuracy 0.688) under the identical protocol, so the comparison is like-for-like. Best sequence-only model seq_combined_gbc (272 phase-resampled + shape features): val 0.709 (-0.117), test 0.662 (-0.017). Adding sequence features on top of the aggregated vector (augmented_gbc, 306 features) is worse on both splits: val 0.770 (-0.055), test 0.517 (-0.162).",
    "evidence": [
      "Overfitting: the best sequence model reaches training macro-F1 1.000 while validation macro-F1 is 0.709 (gap 0.291). With 569 training reps from 16 subjects, 272 sequence features let gradient boosting memorise the training subjects instead of learning form. The aggregated baseline shows the same train/val gap but starts from 34 features, so it loses less to it.",
      "Redundancy: the aggregated vector is not 'temporally blind'. It already carries elbow angular velocity (mean/max), down/up velocity, ROM, rep and phase durations, pause duration and stability statistics \u2014 all derived from the same per-frame signals the sequences are built from. The extra shape detail mostly adds parameters, not information.",
      "Separation is not better: the strongest sequence feature separates the classes by |d|=0.630 versus 0.623 for the strongest aggregated feature. The larger count of sequence features above |d|=0.5 is an artefact of resampling: adjacent phase points of one channel (hip_angle) are near-duplicates, not independent evidence.",
      "Subject sensitivity: on validation the sequence model collapses on a single subject (014: baseline 0.909 -> sequence 0.424), which is exactly the per-subject failure mode documented in ml/scripts/diagnose_generalisation.py. Validation subject-level score is 1 win / 3 losses against the baseline.",
      "Test is inconclusive on its own: the pooled test macro-F1 difference is -0.017, inside the 0.02 noise band, and the sequence model wins on 3 of 4 test subjects \u2014 but by tiny margins, while losing 0.125 accuracy on subject 016. With 4 test subjects this is not enough to claim an improvement. Validation, which is the split used for model selection, is unambiguous and favours the baseline."
